.IX Title "X509_CHECK_CERTIFICATE_TIMES 3ossl"
.TH X509_CHECK_CERTIFICATE_TIMES 3ossl "2026-04-14" "4.0.0" "OpenSSL"
.\" For nroff, turn off justification.  Always turn off hyphenation; it makes
.\" way too many mistakes in technical documents.
.if n .ad l
.nh
.SH "NAME"
X509_check_certificate_times, X509_time_adj, X509_time_adj_ex, X509_gmtime_adj,
X509_cmp_time, X509_cmp_current_time, X509_cmp_timeframe \- X509 time functions
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
.Vb 7
\& int X509_check_certificate_times(const X509_VERIFY_PARAM *vpm, const X509 *x,
\&                                  int *error);
\& ASN1_TIME *X509_time_adj(ASN1_TIME *asn1_time, long offset_sec,
\&                          const time_t *in_tm);
\& ASN1_TIME *X509_time_adj_ex(ASN1_TIME *asn1_time, int offset_day, long
\&                             offset_sec, const time_t *in_tm);
\& ASN1_TIME *X509_gmtime_adj(ASN1_TIME *asn1_time, long offset_sec);
.Ve
.PP
The following functions have been deprecated since OpenSSL 4.0, and can be
hidden entirely by defining \fB\s-1OPENSSL_API_COMPAT\s0\fR with a suitable version value,
see \fIopenssl_user_macros\fR\|(7):
.PP
.Vb 4
\& int X509_cmp_time(const ASN1_TIME *asn1_time, const time_t *in_tm);
\& int X509_cmp_current_time(const ASN1_TIME *asn1_time);
\& int X509_cmp_timeframe(const X509_VERIFY_PARAM *vpm,
\&                        const ASN1_TIME *start, const ASN1_TIME *end);
.Ve
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
\&\fIX509_check_certificate_times()\fR compares the notBefore and notAfter
times in certificate \fIx\fR to check the certificate for temporal validity.
The time used for the check will be the current system time, unless
The the reference time included in the verification parameter \fIvpm\fR
is non \s-1NULL\s0 and \fIvpm\fR has the flag \fBX509_V_FLAG_USE_CHECK_TIME\fR set.
.PP
The notBefore and notAfter times in the certificate will be accepted
only if they are either the format of a GeneralizedTime
(\s-1YYYYMMDDHHMMSSZ\s0), or a UTCTime (\s-1YYMMDDHHMMSSZ\s0) as per \s-1RFC5280,\s0 with
the exception that the requirement: \*(L"CAs conforming to this profile
\&\s-1MUST\s0 always encode certificate validity dates through the year 2049 as
UTCTime; certificate validity dates in 2050 or later \s-1MUST\s0 be encoded
as GeneralizedTime.\*(R" is not enforced.
.PP
\&\fIX509_cmp_time()\fR compares the \s-1ASN1_TIME\s0 in \fIasn1_time\fR with the time
in <in_tm>.
.PP
\&\fIX509_cmp_current_time()\fR compares the \s-1ASN1_TIME\s0 in
\&\fIasn1_time\fR with the current time, expressed as time_t.
.PP
\&\fIX509_cmp_timeframe()\fR compares the given time period with the reference time
included in the verification parameters \fIvpm\fR if they are not \s-1NULL\s0 and contain
\&\fBX509_V_FLAG_USE_CHECK_TIME\fR; else the current time is used as reference time.
.PP
\&\fIX509_time_adj_ex()\fR sets the \s-1ASN1_TIME\s0 structure \fIasn1_time\fR to the time
\&\fIoffset_day\fR and \fIoffset_sec\fR after \fIin_tm\fR.
.PP
\&\fIX509_time_adj()\fR sets the \s-1ASN1_TIME\s0 structure \fIasn1_time\fR to the time
\&\fIoffset_sec\fR after \fIin_tm\fR. This method can only handle second
offsets up to the capacity of long, so the newer \fIX509_time_adj_ex()\fR
\&\s-1API\s0 should be preferred.
.PP
In both methods, if \fIasn1_time\fR is \s-1NULL,\s0 a new \s-1ASN1_TIME\s0 structure
is allocated and returned.
.PP
In all methods, if \fIin_tm\fR is \s-1NULL,\s0 the current time, expressed as
time_t, is used.
.PP
\&\fIasn1_time\fR must satisfy the \s-1ASN1_TIME\s0 format mandated by \s-1RFC 5280,\s0
i.e., its format must be either \s-1YYMMDDHHMMSSZ\s0 or \s-1YYYYMMDDHHMMSSZ.\s0
.PP
\&\fIX509_gmtime_adj()\fR sets the \s-1ASN1_TIME\s0 structure \fIasn1_time\fR to the time
\&\fIoffset_sec\fR after the current time. It is equivalent to calling
\&\fIX509_time_adj()\fR with the last parameter as \s-1NULL.\s0
.SH "BUGS"
.IX Header "BUGS"
Unlike many standard comparison functions, The deprecated functions
\&\fIX509_cmp_time()\fR and \fIX509_cmp_current_time()\fR return 0 on error, and
return \-1 when the values are equal.
.PP
The deprecated function \fIX509_cmp_timeframe()\fR may accept invalid
certificate times as infinitely valid.
.SH "RETURN VALUES"
.IX Header "RETURN VALUES"
\&\fIX509_check_certiticate_times()\fR returns 1 if the certificate is
temporally valid at the verification time as per the rules from \s-1RFC
5280.\s0 It returns 0 otherwise. if \fIerror\fR is non \s-1NULL,\s0 the integer
value it points to will be set to an error code when the certificate
is not temporally valid, or 0 when the certificate is temporally valid.
.PP
The integer pointed to by \fIerror\fR will be set to
X509_V_ERR_ERROR_IN_CERT_NOT_BEFORE_FIELD
or
X509_V_ERR_ERROR_IN_CERT_NOT_AFTER_FIELD
if the certificate has an invalid notBefore or notAfter field, respectively.
.PP
The integer pointed to by \fIerror\fR will be set to
X509_V_ERR_CERT_NOT_YET_VALID
or
X509_V_ERR_CERT_HAS_EXPIRED
if the
verification time is outside of the certificate's correctly encoded
validity window as per \s-1RFC5280.\s0
.PP
\&\fIX509_cmp_time()\fR and \fIX509_cmp_current_time()\fR return \-1 if \fIasn1_time\fR
is earlier than, or equal to, \fIin_tm\fR (resp. current time), and 1
otherwise. These methods return 0 on error.
.PP
\&\fIX509_cmp_timeframe()\fR compares a reference time to a start and end
\&\s-1ASN1_TIME\s0 value range. The reference time is compared to the start
value of the range inclusively, and to the end value of the range
exclusively. The reference time used will retrieved from \fIvpm\fR if the
flag \fI509_V_FLAG_USE_CHECK_TIME\fR is set in \fIvpm\fR, otherwise it will
be the current time. The start time used will be \fIstart\fR if it is non
\&\s-1NULL,\s0 and a valid \s-1ASN1_TIME\s0 value, otherwise it will be infinitely in
the past. The end time used will be \fIend\fR if it is non \s-1NULL\s0 and a
valid \s-1ASN1_TIME\s0 value, otherwise it will be infinitely in the
future. 0 is returned unconditionally if the flag
\&\fIX509_V_FLAG_NO_CHECK_TIME\fR is set in \fIvpm\fR. Otherwise 0 will be
returned if the reference time is in the range of the start time and
end time as described above. 1 is returned to indicate the reference
time is after or equal to the end time. \-1 is returned to indicate the
reference time is before the start time. As this function treats
invalid \s-1ASN1_TIME\s0 inputs as unlimited times, it should not be used to
for temporal verification of certificates using untrusted inputs that
have not been pre-validated to be correct \s-1ASN1_TIME\s0 values for a
certificate as per \s-1RFC 5280,\s0 as invalid values will be accepted
as valid forever.
.PP
\&\fIX509_time_adj()\fR, \fIX509_time_adj_ex()\fR and \fIX509_gmtime_adj()\fR return a pointer to
the updated \s-1ASN1_TIME\s0 structure, and \s-1NULL\s0 on error.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IX Header "SEE ALSO"
\&\fIASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME_check\fR\|(3)
\&\fIASN1_UTCTIME_check\fR\|(3)
\&\fIASN1_TIME_to_tm\fR\|(3)
\&\fIOPENSSL_tm_to_posix\fR\|(3) \fIOPENSSL_posix_to_tm\fR\|(3)
\&\fIX509_verify_cert_error_string\fR\|(3)
.SH "HISTORY"
.IX Header "HISTORY"
\&\fIX509_cmp_timeframe()\fR, \fIX509_cmp_current_time()\fR, and
\&\fIX509_cmp_timeframe()\fR were deprecated in OpenSSL 4.0
.PP
For replacement, consider using \fIX509_check_certificate_times()\fR for use
with X509 certificates. For applications checking individual \s-1ASN1_TIME\s0
values, consider using \fIASN1_TIME_to_tm\fR\|(3) with appropriate validity
checking of the \fI\s-1ASN1_TIME\s0\fR value for your application, and subsequent
comparison of either the resulting \fItm\fR structure, or conversion to
posix seconds via \fIOPENSSL_tm_to_posix\fR\|(3)
.PP
\&\fIX509_check_certificate_times()\fR was added in OpenSSL 4.0.
